{{also|épicycle}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|epicyclus|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|ἐπίκυκλος|lang=grc}}, from {{term|ἐπί||upon|lang=grc}} + {{term|κύκλος||circle|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɛpɪsaɪkəl/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|astronomy|lang=en}} A small [[circle]] whose centre is on the [[circumference]] of a larger circle; in Ptolemaic astronomy it was seen as the basis of revolution of the "seven planets", given a fixed central Earth.
#* '''1603''', John Florio, translating Michel de Montaigne, ''Essays'', Folio Society 2006, vol. 1 p. 155:
#*: Is it not [Philosophie], that [...] teacheth miserie, famine and sicknesse to laugh?  Not by reason of some imaginarie '''Epicicles''', but by naturall and palpable reasons.
# {{context|mathematics|lang=en}} Any circle whose circumference [[roll]]s around that of another circle, thus creating a [[hypocycloid]] or [[epicycloid]].

====Derived terms====
* [[epicyclic]]

